Chapter: SheetViewTest
  cites Sheets chapter CellView
  cites Sheets chapter CellEval
  cites Sheets chapter CellStore
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 The sheet a person looks at: a store, the formulas over it, and the
 window. The lines below are what a desk pane or a web view would draw,
 so the layout is the assertion.

 What each part holds. Numbers are right-aligned and text is left, which
 is the only reason a column of quantities can be read down. A NUMBER
 TOO WIDE fills with `#` rather than being cut, because a cut number
 reads as a different number and nothing in the display says so; text
 IS cut, with a `>` in the last position, because a cut label is still
 recognisably that label and the mark is visible. The refusal line shows
 what a typed refusal looks like in a cell: a sentence, laid out as
 text, cut like any other text.

 The window lines hold that the view offsets rather than the data
 moving: the same sheet drawn at top 1, left 1 shows B2 and C2 in the
 first cell positions and the row numbers still read 2 and 3.

 The bar line holds the thing an editor needs: the cell a person is on
 shows what was TYPED, which for a formula cell is the formula and not
 its answer.

 The narrow window is four rows deep on purpose. Three rows of it hold
 250, 400, 750 and 800, every one of which FITS in a column three wide,
 so a `#` rule could be missing entirely and those rows would look the
 same; the fourth row holds 1,550, which does not fit, and is the only
 line in the file that can fail (L-VACUOUS).

 The sabotage: right-align text and the first column stops reading as
 labels; cut a number instead of filling with `#` and the narrow view
 prints 155 where the value is 1,550, a plausible number that is wrong.

Section: Body

  sv-nl : Text
  sv-nl = utf8-bytes-to-text [10]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let s0 = sheet-open 6 4 512
    in let s1 = sheet-type (sheet-type s0 0 0 "Item") 0 1 "Qty"
    in let s2 = sheet-type (sheet-type s1 0 2 "Cost") 0 3 "Total"
    in let s3 = sheet-type (sheet-type s2 1 0 "Widget") 1 1 "3"
    in let s4 = sheet-type (sheet-type s3 1 2 "250") 1 3 "=B2*C2"
    in let s5 = sheet-type (sheet-type s4 2 0 "Gadget") 2 1 "2"
    in let s6 = sheet-type (sheet-type s5 2 2 "400") 2 3 "=B3*C3"
    in let s7 = sheet-type (sheet-type s6 3 0 "Total") 3 3 "=SUM(D2:D3)"
    in let s8 = sheet-type (sheet-type s7 4 0 "Refusal") 4 3 "=B2+A2"
    in let narrow = sheet-look s8 0 2 4 2 3
    in let window = sheet-look s8 1 1 2 2 9
    in act
      print-line-uni "the sheet as a person sees it:"
      print-line-uni (sheet-render s8)
      print-line-uni "a column too narrow: a number fills with # and text is cut with >"
      print-line-uni (sheet-render narrow)
      print-line-uni "the window offsets, and the row numbers stay with their rows:"
      print-line-uni (sheet-render window)
      print-line-uni ("the bar on a formula cell: " & sheet-bar s8 3 3)
      print-line-uni ("the bar on a text cell: " & sheet-bar s8 0 0)
      print-line-uni ("the bar on a number cell: " & sheet-bar s8 1 1)
      print-line-uni ("typing nothing clears a cell: " & sheet-bar (sheet-type s8 1 1 "") 1 1)
    end
